\title{The detector branch: interpolation to full-rank orders}
\taxon{Overview}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Expository organization and source review, 9 October 2026.}

\p{The detector must do three things at once: kill the additive ambiguity, charge at most one rank per crossing contribution, and give full rank for every vertex order. Barycentric cancellation gives the first property; rank-one atoms give the second; two-triangle interpolation gives the third. The [four-by-four example](cn-0040) makes the construction concrete.}

